1
00:00:00,174 --> 00:00:00,960
Hi everyone!

2
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:06,340
Welcome to this video, and this will be a full walkthrough of my agentic engineering workflow.

3
00:00:06,766 --> 00:00:07,440
My name is Kun.

4
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:18,020
I was previously an L8 principal engineer, worked at Meta, Microsoft, and Atlassian on many large-scale systems like the Bing Search Engine, Windows, and Facebook games.

5
00:00:18,382 --> 00:00:27,040
In the recent couple of years, I have been building frontier coding agents at Atlassian and helped many engineering teams figure out how to use them effectively.

6
00:00:27,278 --> 00:00:33,924
And I have been building heavily with agents myself and shipping 40 to 50 PRs almost every day, sometimes more.

7
00:00:34,414 --> 00:00:40,800
And these are all well-tested and shipped production, not those Minecraft demos you see people vibecode on social media.

8
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:45,156
I have shaped my workflow to be both highly productive and enjoyable.

9
00:00:45,646 --> 00:00:47,920
Many people recently asked me what it looks like.

10
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:56,132
To be honest, I did debate a lot with myself whether I should make this video a paid course because it does have that level of value.

11
00:00:56,462 --> 00:01:02,852
But ultimately I decided to just share it here with everyone because I want to stay focused on building products as my main business.

12
00:01:03,182 --> 00:01:15,280
You can see this is a bit of a long video because I'm going to walk through many fundamental concepts of agentic engineering that not only show you how I do it, but also the why and how things really work under the hood.

13
00:01:15,502 --> 00:01:19,524
These are not gimmicks that look cool but can't actually be used for real work.

14
00:01:19,854 --> 00:01:25,188
These are all real workflows that professionals like myself use to get real work done.

15
00:01:25,390 --> 00:01:35,760
By the end of this video, I want you to feel like a captain that can sail a large ship with a crew of agents working for you, and do so in a stress-free and satisfying way.

16
00:01:36,078 --> 00:01:39,012
Largely speaking, we will be walking through these chapters.

17
00:01:39,822 --> 00:01:44,676
We'll start with assembling our ship, where I will introduce the core setup.

18
00:01:45,262 --> 00:01:51,972
We will then talk through how we recruit and ramp up our crewmates with the right usage of memory and skills.

19
00:01:52,942 --> 00:01:56,996
I will then demonstrate how we work with a single crewmate effectively.

20
00:01:57,390 --> 00:02:01,220
Then we'll upgrade to working with multiple crewmates all at the same time.

21
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:10,720
And lastly, we will recruit a first mate that manages a lot of the overhead for us so we can stay focused on the big picture as a captain.

22
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:14,880
The very first level is to gather our gears and build our ship.

23
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:22,404
Now, as we get into my workflow, something that's going to be really hard to miss is that I do almost everything in my terminal.

24
00:02:22,990 --> 00:02:27,396
I know there are a lot of people who will tell you that the graphical user interface is better.

25
00:02:27,662 --> 00:02:30,404
It allows richer interactions and better visuals.

26
00:02:30,894 --> 00:02:37,252
But I think by the end of this video, I might just be able to convince you that terminal is not quite dead yet.

27
00:02:38,094 --> 00:02:40,900
I use the terminal mostly for two very real reasons.

28
00:02:41,422 --> 00:02:45,188
One is to allow my hands to almost never have to leave the keyboard.

29
00:02:45,646 --> 00:02:52,900
This is actually a much bigger deal than most people think, because when your hands stay on the keyboard, you stay in the flow.

30
00:02:53,358 --> 00:03:00,740
But if you have to move your hand to the mouse every couple of seconds, it breaks the flow and forces your brain to contact switch.

31
00:03:01,102 --> 00:03:17,988
I know there are some GUI apps that also have great keybinds that allow you to do most things with the keyboard as well, but that's just not the primary interaction paradigm for GUI apps, and it's hard to build the discipline of hands-on keyboards when every once in a while you still have to use the mouse.

32
00:03:18,766 --> 00:03:22,340
Terminal apps, on the other hand, are all designed for the keyboard.

33
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:25,732
So there's no reason for your hands to move anywhere else.

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,572
The other very important factor that drives me to use the terminal is that I can keep the exact same workflow everywhere, even on my phone.

35
00:03:33,774 --> 00:03:36,932
But if you really don't like the terminal, that's okay too.

36
00:03:37,230 --> 00:03:43,812
I designed this video to be more about the fundamental concepts behind agentic engineering rather than the mechanics.

37
00:03:44,270 --> 00:03:49,572
So most of the things that I talk about should be applicable to GUI-based workflows as well.

38
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:54,468
Now, since we are looking at a terminal here, let me share what it is I'm using.

39
00:03:54,958 --> 00:04:00,868
This beautiful, clean, and elegant terminal emulator you are looking at here is called Westterm.

40
00:04:01,838 --> 00:04:06,084
Westterm is a highly performance terminal emulator built by a guy named Wes.

41
00:04:06,414 --> 00:04:10,564
It's got 26k GitHub stars and has existed for many years.

42
00:04:11,054 --> 00:04:12,804
I like it mostly for two reasons.

43
00:04:13,262 --> 00:04:15,492
One is that it's truly cross platform.

44
00:04:15,886 --> 00:04:22,820
It's pretty much the only terminal emulator I can find that can work on Windows exactly the same way it works on Mac and Linux.

45
00:04:23,630 --> 00:04:31,300
Right now I mostly only work on Mac, but it was a big lifesaver when I was working for Microsoft and was forced to use Windows for work.

46
00:04:32,142 --> 00:04:34,756
The other reason is that it's highly customizable.

47
00:04:35,022 --> 00:04:38,128
You can write Lua scripts to configure pretty much everything.

48
00:04:38,414 --> 00:04:40,708
Here, let me show you my config in my files.

49
00:04:41,294 --> 00:04:44,164
It's all in this file called Westterm.lua.

50
00:04:44,288 --> 00:04:47,172
It's a Lua script, so it's not just static values.

51
00:04:47,408 --> 00:04:53,412
You can actually set conditions and write various kinds of logic to make your config very dynamic and flexible.

52
00:04:54,062 --> 00:05:04,068
If I change some settings here, let's say I change the color scheme to chalk, you will see that it does a hot reload instantly, which is super handy.

53
00:05:04,526 --> 00:05:07,492
But I still like my Rosepine Moon, so let's come back to it.

54
00:05:07,854 --> 00:05:09,028
I can't use anything else.

55
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:13,060
Inside of West Term, I run something called Tmux.

56
00:05:13,518 --> 00:05:15,652
It's short for terminal multiplexer.

57
00:05:16,142 --> 00:05:20,208
If you haven't come across this yet, it's probably easiest to just show you what this does.

58
00:05:20,462 --> 00:05:23,748
So I'm typing this command here to start a tmux session.

59
00:05:24,366 --> 00:05:25,860
Now I'm inside of Tmux.

60
00:05:26,382 --> 00:05:32,164
You can see not much is different except for that there is a bar at the top showing some information.

61
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:35,236
And I still get a shell where I can type commands.

62
00:05:35,568 --> 00:05:40,772
But now I can split my terminal into multiple panes, as many of them as I like.

63
00:05:41,198 --> 00:05:47,620
This is super useful because I can spin up an agent in one pane and spin up an editor in another.

64
00:05:48,128 --> 00:05:52,132
And still have a pane to myself so I can just run commands.

65
00:05:52,462 --> 00:05:57,124
I can also spin up multiple tabs and they are also called windows in TMUX.

66
00:05:57,422 --> 00:06:00,708
This is very useful for running multiple agent sessions in parallel.

67
00:06:01,326 --> 00:06:05,988
The other cool thing is that TMUX sessions are persistent in the Tmux server.

68
00:06:06,414 --> 00:06:14,852
So if I use a keyboard shortcut here to detach from Tmux, you can see I'm back in the normal shell without the Tmux status bar at the top.

69
00:06:15,278 --> 00:06:21,968
But if I type the same command to launch Tmux again, I get back to the exact same state I was in before.

70
00:06:22,128 --> 00:06:23,748
So I can continue my work here.

71
00:06:24,238 --> 00:06:30,564
What's even more useful is that I can connect to this same session from another device, like my laptop or my phone.

72
00:06:30,958 --> 00:06:36,288
That's a real game changer that's very hard to replicate without this terminal-centric workflow.

73
00:06:36,846 --> 00:06:43,940
If you just install Tmux, by default it doesn't have the same experience we're showing here, like the tab bar and the metadata.

74
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:47,972
You'll probably need to do a bit of configuration and customize it.

75
00:06:48,334 --> 00:06:50,436
Let me show you my tmux config.

76
00:06:52,750 --> 00:06:53,252
Here it is.

77
00:06:53,710 --> 00:06:59,652
Most of these settings are key binds that I have been using for many years and built into my muscle memory.

78
00:07:00,238 --> 00:07:03,568
Some of these are for styling and various kinds of behaviors.

79
00:07:03,758 --> 00:07:10,500
There are many YouTube videos that go into more details about TMUX configuration, so I'm not going to go down the rabbit hole here.

80
00:07:10,926 --> 00:07:18,688
For now, you just need to know that you are likely want to spend some time configuring your TMUX for it to look good and work well for you.

81
00:07:18,862 --> 00:07:20,836
This text editor here is Neo Vim.

82
00:07:21,358 --> 00:07:23,300
It's basically the modern version of Vim.

83
00:07:23,728 --> 00:07:25,412
It's my favorite text editor.

84
00:07:25,838 --> 00:07:31,620
If you are not familiar with Vim yet, it's an editor whose main purpose is to keep your hands on the keyboard.

85
00:07:32,078 --> 00:07:38,148
So if you watch my keystrokes here, I can move the cursor up and down, left and right with keys.

86
00:07:38,798 --> 00:07:41,508
I can also scroll up or scroll down.

87
00:07:42,062 --> 00:07:47,236
If I have to make edits, I can go into insert mode and start to type anything I like.

88
00:07:48,398 --> 00:07:51,488
There are a ton of keyboard shortcuts for doing everything you need.

89
00:07:51,758 --> 00:07:56,612
For example, let's say I want to delete the current line, I can just type DD and it's gone.

90
00:07:57,166 --> 00:07:58,928
I can undo it by typing U.

91
00:07:59,118 --> 00:08:03,044
Oh, and if you look at the left hand side, I have relative line numbers.

92
00:08:03,568 --> 00:08:07,556
This line number 238 is the current line number.

93
00:08:07,982 --> 00:08:14,244
And the line above shows 1, which means it's one line above the current line, and the lines below as well.

94
00:08:14,798 --> 00:08:18,724
So let's say I want to jump to the line that says set environment.

95
00:08:19,406 --> 00:08:21,728
That's 11 lines above the current line.

96
00:08:21,808 --> 00:08:24,644
So I can just type 11k and I'm here.

97
00:08:25,326 --> 00:08:30,692
So once you have enough muscle memory, you can just navigate around much more quickly than using a mouse.

98
00:08:31,278 --> 00:08:35,364
I also have a bunch of plugins that help me get around in NeoVim as well.

99
00:08:35,888 --> 00:08:37,732
And I have keybinds for all of them.

100
00:08:38,158 --> 00:08:41,860
Like Space S allows me to search or grab for the codebase.

101
00:08:42,190 --> 00:08:47,588
So I can just type rows and it'll find all the occurrences of rows in the current codebase.

102
00:08:48,206 --> 00:08:51,888
I can type Space F to find files by their names.

103
00:08:51,968 --> 00:08:55,968
Like if I type Flake, I'll get to the Flake file immediately.

104
00:08:56,430 --> 00:09:02,848
Working with Vim has a learning curve for sure, but once you get used to it, it just feels really really good.

105
00:09:03,246 --> 00:09:07,168
Whenever I'm in NeoVim, I'm just flying like a bird and it's awesome.

106
00:09:07,328 --> 00:09:10,596
Okay, I have to stop here before this turns into a Vim tutorial.

107
00:09:10,926 --> 00:09:16,324
You can find a lot of great YouTube videos that will help you get started on Vim and become a master.

108
00:09:16,878 --> 00:09:19,908
Maybe one last tip from me is just how to exit.

109
00:09:20,208 --> 00:09:20,932
Here you go.

110
00:09:21,838 --> 00:09:25,540
Alright, our ship is ready to sail, but we have no crewmates yet.

111
00:09:26,126 --> 00:09:31,172
We're the captain, we can't do everything by ourselves, we need to bring in agents as our crewmates.

112
00:09:31,918 --> 00:09:34,320
I use four different agent harnesses regularly.

113
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:41,892
There is Claude, which is uh Claude Code, which is basically the only practical choice if you are using the subscription from Anthropic.

114
00:09:42,510 --> 00:09:44,960
Generally speaking though, it's a pretty good harness.

115
00:09:45,326 --> 00:09:48,964
I think it has the most sensible default experience out of the box.

116
00:09:49,550 --> 00:09:51,600
It's also got a pretty rich feature set.

117
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:58,340
The downside is that sometimes it's a little bit buggy and it's not as customizable as some of the other options.

118
00:09:59,086 --> 00:10:01,828
The next one I use a lot is Codex COI.

119
00:10:02,382 --> 00:10:07,332
It's written in Rust, and you can feel it's a little bit smoother than Cloud Code when you use it.

120
00:10:07,982 --> 00:10:16,388
It's also open source, so if you run into some problems, you can often just have Codex inspect its own source code and figure out a workaround by itself.

121
00:10:17,230 --> 00:10:21,988
It's a bit lacking in terms of bells and whistles, and it's also not very customizable.

122
00:10:22,830 --> 00:10:25,092
And then there is the Pi coding agent.

123
00:10:25,742 --> 00:10:29,600
And its whole philosophy is to be minimal and highly extensible.

124
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:35,268
It's great if you don't want any bloat and you'd like to tinker around and kind of make it your own.

125
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:37,636
And lastly, there is open code.

126
00:10:38,958 --> 00:10:39,908
I like it a lot.

127
00:10:40,174 --> 00:10:46,436
It's got a buttery smooth TUI and it's got good integration with pretty much every model you can find.

128
00:10:46,990 --> 00:10:59,520
It's also got a more complete out-of-the-box feature set than Pi, so if you want to use an agent harness that is model agnostic and one that you can just grab from the shelf and just go, open code is a pretty good choice.

129
00:10:59,758 --> 00:11:12,560
For the rest of this video though, I'm going to use Cloud Code because I know many people are already familiar with it, but I have been very strict about making my workflow agent agnostic because the landscape is changing very very fast.

130
00:11:12,750 --> 00:11:16,640
Who knows which model or agent will be the best performing one next month, right?

131
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:24,228
So everything I show here in the video is agent agnostic and should be applicable regardless of which model or harness you use.

132
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:32,100
Now, the problem with these crewmates is that they are fresh recruits and they have no idea how we run our ship or how we like to work.

133
00:11:32,622 --> 00:11:35,428
We need a proper onboarding process to ramp them up.

134
00:11:36,046 --> 00:11:39,684
We'll do this mostly through two ways memory files and skills.

135
00:11:40,430 --> 00:11:45,476
There are a few types of memory files, global memory files and project level memory files.

136
00:11:46,062 --> 00:11:49,732
The global memory file for clot code is at this location.

137
00:11:51,790 --> 00:11:55,940
And every other agent uses the other standard location here.

138
00:11:57,102 --> 00:12:07,780
So what I do is that I use this command, which made clot.md a symbolic link to agents.md, so they both exist, but under the hood they point to the same file.

139
00:12:08,526 --> 00:12:12,036
Here's the content of my actual global memory file.

140
00:12:12,398 --> 00:12:23,172
You can see it's pretty minimal, there's only 27 lines, because everything in this file gets loaded into the system prompt of every single agent session across all our projects.

141
00:12:23,854 --> 00:12:28,740
If we have too much content in this file, it will silently use a lot of our tokens.

142
00:12:29,422 --> 00:12:33,430
I mostly write down my personal preferences here, like never use m.-dash.

143
00:12:34,478 --> 00:12:39,332
Somehow AI models are trained to use m dash by default instead of a plain dash.

144
00:12:39,694 --> 00:12:42,980
So now whenever I see m dash, I just feel like it's robotic.

145
00:12:43,406 --> 00:12:47,908
And I don't like that when I need the agent to write something for me, like PR descriptions.

146
00:12:48,142 --> 00:12:49,252
Oh and this is a good one.

147
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:54,052
When making technical decisions, don't give too much weight to development cost.

148
00:12:54,350 --> 00:12:56,196
Here's something interesting that you may not know.

149
00:12:56,400 --> 00:12:57,600
Let me show you.

150
00:12:58,286 --> 00:13:12,036
If we ask a frontier model to estimate the development cost of a project, let's say I want to build a 3D first person shooting game that I can play locally with AI enemies.

151
00:13:12,718 --> 00:13:15,268
How long do you think that will take?

152
00:13:16,334 --> 00:13:17,924
Let's see what Claude will say.

153
00:13:18,638 --> 00:13:19,492
Okay, here it is.

154
00:13:20,110 --> 00:13:22,884
See the estimate is in days and weeks and months.

155
00:13:23,662 --> 00:13:30,788
But if we ask the agent to actually build it now, I can guarantee it will come back with a playable version in just a few minutes.

156
00:13:31,310 --> 00:13:33,060
Because I have done this so many times.

157
00:13:33,646 --> 00:13:38,404
This mismatch is happening because the models were trained from human data.

158
00:13:38,766 --> 00:13:42,724
And that is what a typical human developer would give as the estimate.

159
00:13:43,438 --> 00:13:46,852
AI doesn't seem to know it can code much faster than humans yet.

160
00:13:47,214 --> 00:13:56,580
So when AI is making technical decisions, it's implicitly assuming the development cost for some of the options are much higher than they actually are.

161
00:13:57,134 --> 00:14:04,452
This biases the model to choose cheap solutions that are often low quality, not scalable, or hard to maintain.

162
00:14:05,166 --> 00:14:07,972
So I have this rule here to correct that bias.

163
00:14:09,326 --> 00:14:21,280
I also said when doing bug fixes, always start with reproducing the bug in an end-to-end setting as closely aligned with how an end user would experience it as possible.

164
00:14:21,518 --> 00:14:29,956
AI models today, by default, like to write unit tests, which are often not sufficient and not really covering the product behaviors we want to guard.

165
00:14:30,574 --> 00:14:34,160
I found that leaning into end-to-end testing is a lot more reliable.

166
00:14:34,414 --> 00:14:40,740
Besides these preferences, I also have some interesting stuff like opinions.md, which is super useful.

167
00:14:41,262 --> 00:14:44,708
That's a slightly different topic though, so I won't go into too much detail here.

168
00:14:44,910 --> 00:14:49,732
But I do have a blog post explaining how that works, which I'll link here in case you're interested.

169
00:14:50,030 --> 00:14:54,596
Besides the global memory file, each project can also have a project level memory file.

170
00:14:54,958 --> 00:14:58,692
Let me show you one example here by going into this project called HiBit.

171
00:14:59,150 --> 00:15:01,476
This is an AI Twitter app I have been working on.

172
00:15:02,030 --> 00:15:08,880
The project level memory file is typically stored as Cloud.md or agents.md, depending on which agent you use.

173
00:15:09,198 --> 00:15:14,820
I did the same thing here with a symbolic link, so the same file is shared for both Cloud and other agents.

174
00:15:15,790 --> 00:15:18,852
This one we're looking at here is a little bit verbose, I would say.

175
00:15:19,214 --> 00:15:24,292
I'll probably clean this up after this, but let me show you on a high level what I put into this file.

176
00:15:24,846 --> 00:15:40,068
It has some context on what this project is, how the repo is laid out, some terminology, how some of the most important components work, and how to do end-to-end testing, and some conventions at the bottom.

177
00:15:41,006 --> 00:15:48,804
This file is a lot more verbose than the global memory file, because this is basically the collective learning of all the agent sessions in this project.

178
00:15:49,102 --> 00:16:03,108
The way I built this file is not by writing everything by hand, but rather that every time I saw the agent doing something wrong, I would correct it and ask it to remember to not make the same mistake again by storing the learning into this memory file.

179
00:16:03,280 --> 00:16:07,652
So over time, our crewmates working on this project get smarter and more experienced.

180
00:16:08,270 --> 00:16:10,560
You don't need any fancy memory system to do that.

181
00:16:10,734 --> 00:16:12,388
This markdown file is all it takes.

182
00:16:12,974 --> 00:16:16,356
Over time, it does tend to get more and more bloated though.

183
00:16:16,720 --> 00:16:23,930
One way I reduce the size of this file is by moving some conditional information that is not always needed into a skill.

184
00:16:24,686 --> 00:16:31,684
For example, the end-to-end testing instruction here is only needed if the agent is making changes, right?

185
00:16:32,206 --> 00:16:38,468
So if I just ask the agent a question, this whole section is totally useless and would be wasting tokens.

186
00:16:39,150 --> 00:16:46,756
The way to improve efficiency here is by converting this kind of conditionally useful information from the memory file into skills.

187
00:16:47,310 --> 00:16:49,412
I typically just ask the agent to do this.

188
00:16:49,760 --> 00:16:51,332
here, let me do it live.

189
00:16:52,238 --> 00:17:05,280
I would say let's extract the end to end testing instructions in our agents.md file into a project level skill.

190
00:17:07,502 --> 00:17:12,480
Claude already knew how to do this, what skills mean and how to create them.

191
00:17:12,750 --> 00:17:17,200
But other agent harnesses may not understand how to do that out of the box.

192
00:17:17,582 --> 00:17:25,636
To teach your agent how to create skills, you can install a skill called Skill Creator, which was written by Anthropic.

193
00:17:26,080 --> 00:17:27,940
You can do that by running this command.

194
00:17:28,942 --> 00:17:34,180
This NPX skills thing is a COI from Vercel that is very handy.

195
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:38,468
It's basically my main tool for installing and managing skills.

196
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:40,708
It supports pretty much any agent.

197
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:47,108
Once this skill is installed, your agent will be able to follow the rules and create new skills for you moving forward.

198
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:49,200
All right, Cloud has done its work.

199
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:51,908
Let's look at what Cloud created for us.

200
00:17:53,230 --> 00:18:04,612
It basically removed a large chunk of the content from our agents.md file and moved that into this skill file.

201
00:18:05,550 --> 00:18:18,564
This is a good thing about skills is that it's designed for progressive disclosure, which means when your agent starts, it only loads this tiny description field from your skills into the system prompt to know what these skills do.

202
00:18:19,406 --> 00:18:25,572
And only when it actually decides that it needs to use a certain skill, it will then read the rest of this file.

203
00:18:26,062 --> 00:18:39,876
This allows you to store a lot of the knowledge about how to do various kinds of things without blowing up your system prompt and memory file with a ton of content that uses your tokens for every single request, whether the request actually needs those skills or not.

204
00:18:40,302 --> 00:18:49,988
One thing I do want you to know about skills is that you should generally avoid installing random skills from the internet, even the ones that have a lot of GitHub stars.

205
00:18:50,830 --> 00:18:55,684
First of all, these skills can instruct your agent to run pretty much anything on your machine.

206
00:18:56,110 --> 00:19:05,680
This is a very risky thing to do, because the agent can leak your API keys or even credentials to your bank account to untrusted third parties without you knowing.

207
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:11,844
And even if we put aside the security problem, some of the skills actually degrade your agent's performance.

208
00:19:12,558 --> 00:19:19,844
Look at this repo here called Andre Caparthy Skills, which has 177,000 GitHub stars.

209
00:19:20,110 --> 00:19:23,236
That's like massive, so it must be really good, right?

210
00:19:23,854 --> 00:19:32,080
I actually evaluated the skill in this repo with Programbench, which tests the agent's ability to build programs end-to-end.

211
00:19:32,686 --> 00:19:40,580
And the result shows that by using this skill, the agent will use 5% more tokens while making the results worse.

212
00:19:41,486 --> 00:19:46,756
And if you look closely, this skill is not even written by Andre Capati himself.

213
00:19:47,630 --> 00:19:55,396
I'm not here to criticize the author of this repo though, I'm mainly saying that being popular is not the same as actually being good.

214
00:19:55,950 --> 00:20:08,640
A lot of the skills being widely shared today have not been rigorously evaluated and are typically just some random guy who found something that worked for themselves anecdotally and somehow got it to go viral.

215
00:20:08,910 --> 00:20:14,372
Their GitHub stars only tell you how popular they are and not whether they are actually helpful.

216
00:20:14,894 --> 00:20:27,460
So as a general rule of thumb, I recommend that you do not install any skill from the internet that claims to magically make your agent perform better but hasn't published anything rigorous that proves its claim.

217
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:36,516
Alright, now that we have memory files and skills to help ramp up crewmates, it's finally time to actually start working with a crewmate and set sail.

218
00:20:36,750 --> 00:20:40,228
The first thing about working with a crewmate is how you talk to them.

219
00:20:40,814 --> 00:20:43,844
I have pretty much completely moved to voice input now.

220
00:20:44,046 --> 00:20:52,100
So instead of typing, I would just say, explain this repo in a concise way and give me a recap of what the recent PRs have been working on.

221
00:20:53,390 --> 00:20:54,544
This is just so easy.

222
00:20:54,704 --> 00:21:03,780
There is an actual paper from Stanford that seriously compared the efficiency, and basically, talking is three times faster than typing.

223
00:21:04,334 --> 00:21:06,660
So this is a very big boost in productivity.

224
00:21:07,406 --> 00:21:09,412
I also want to show you something interesting here.

225
00:21:09,744 --> 00:21:14,532
If we go to the references of this paper, look who's here.

226
00:21:15,086 --> 00:21:16,484
It's our guy Dario.

227
00:21:17,102 --> 00:21:19,364
What is the CEO of Anthropic doing here?

228
00:21:20,142 --> 00:21:27,780
Apparently, if we follow this link, Dario was doing some speech recognition stuff back in 2016.

229
00:21:28,078 --> 00:21:33,504
Now we're using speech recognition technology to talk to Claude, which is also created by Dario.

230
00:21:33,966 --> 00:21:35,076
What a small world.

231
00:21:36,046 --> 00:21:42,564
The voice input we just did was actually transcribed locally using this app called OpenSilper Whisper.

232
00:21:43,150 --> 00:21:47,492
It's completely free and open source, which is what I think this type of software should be.

233
00:21:47,726 --> 00:21:51,684
It runs the Whisper model locally on your machine and do the transcription.

234
00:21:52,206 --> 00:21:54,052
And the quality is like really really good.

235
00:21:54,830 --> 00:21:57,060
So this is how I do most of my prompts now.

236
00:21:57,518 --> 00:22:04,996
The only case where I fall back to typing is when I need to give the agent a URL or a file path or something like that.

237
00:22:05,582 --> 00:22:12,068
Trust me, you don't want to speak a URL out loud, whether it's by yourself or with other humans around.

238
00:22:12,782 --> 00:22:23,556
Now, if we come back to this prompt and let the agent run, you will see that because we asked the agent to look at recent PRs, it will need to call GitHub to fetch the data.

239
00:22:24,206 --> 00:22:31,268
This is an important thing to pay attention to, because agents rely on external tools like GitHub to do its tasks.

240
00:22:31,584 --> 00:22:35,504
The design of these external tools can greatly affect your agent's performance.

241
00:22:35,726 --> 00:22:37,156
Take GitHub as an example.

242
00:22:37,454 --> 00:22:40,804
Many people use the GitHub MCP server for accessing GitHub.

243
00:22:41,390 --> 00:22:47,780
However, I ran this benchmark here that measured various kind of ways to access GitHub.

244
00:22:48,046 --> 00:22:59,588
For the exact same tasks, using GitHub MCP server will cost you to spend three times more on token cost and more than double the latency compared to using the COI.

245
00:23:00,174 --> 00:23:06,212
If you're using the GitHub MCP, you are pretty much wasting both time and money for no clear benefit.

246
00:23:06,384 --> 00:23:12,548
Now you can see there's this thing called Axie, which has the lowest cost but highest success rate.

247
00:23:13,038 --> 00:23:13,860
So what is it?

248
00:23:14,670 --> 00:23:15,428
Let me show you.

249
00:23:16,940 --> 00:23:29,764
Axie is a set of design standards I authored after discovering the huge upside we can have by designing our tools to treat agents as a first-class citizen and optimize for agent ergonomics.

250
00:23:30,158 --> 00:23:35,428
I created 10 principles for how to make a tool highly efficient for agents.

251
00:23:36,174 --> 00:23:42,436
For example, using token efficient output format can save about 40% tokens compared to using JSON.

252
00:23:43,182 --> 00:23:45,184
And then I built a few Axis with them.

253
00:23:45,422 --> 00:23:53,604
Besides the GitHub Axie I showed earlier, I also built Chrome DevTools Axie and benchmarked it against other various browser tools.

254
00:23:55,278 --> 00:24:01,984
Here you can see the agent taking less turns and using less tokens to get the same tasks done with the Axie.

255
00:24:02,224 --> 00:24:11,620
The main point here is when you give tools to your agent, do some research on their efficiency because they can greatly affect how much mileage you get out of your agents.

256
00:24:12,814 --> 00:24:20,036
If you want to use the Axies I mentioned earlier, you can just go to this site called axie.md and find them in this catalog.

257
00:24:20,686 --> 00:24:25,092
You can just go to their repo and find the instructions for how to start using them.

258
00:24:26,158 --> 00:24:30,148
Speaking of this catalog, there is something called lavish Axie here.

259
00:24:30,766 --> 00:24:33,156
This is a very important tool in my setup.

260
00:24:33,550 --> 00:24:37,348
I pretty much rely on this tool for planning any kind of complex work.

261
00:24:37,870 --> 00:24:40,420
Let's do a real feature live and I'll show you how it works.

262
00:24:40,974 --> 00:24:44,676
Let me first launch HiBit to show you what I'm trying to work on here.

263
00:24:45,326 --> 00:24:48,068
HiBit is an AI tutor I'm building for kids.

264
00:24:49,024 --> 00:24:51,364
I'll just create a test profile here.

265
00:24:53,102 --> 00:24:58,916
You can see here at the top, I have these two buttons what I can do and my progress.

266
00:24:59,630 --> 00:25:03,652
They are showing very similar content right now, which is a problem.

267
00:25:04,206 --> 00:25:08,164
So um, and also the UI is not very exciting or fun.

268
00:25:08,718 --> 00:25:11,364
So let me go back and talk to Claude.

269
00:25:14,094 --> 00:25:15,876
I'll still use voice input here.

270
00:25:16,654 --> 00:25:25,888
I'll just say I'd like to consolidate um the what I can do and my progress buttons because their functionality is very similar.

271
00:25:26,158 --> 00:25:33,508
And I'd like to revamp the experience there to be something more fun and exciting, like an achievement system.

272
00:25:34,062 --> 00:25:36,036
Come up with some options and let's discuss.

273
00:25:36,302 --> 00:25:37,412
Don't use lavish.

274
00:25:39,088 --> 00:25:39,408
Okay.

275
00:25:39,982 --> 00:25:49,988
The reason I said don't use lavish is that I wanted to show you what the default workflow today looks like for many people, and then I'm going to show you the difference lavish makes.

276
00:25:50,368 --> 00:25:53,060
Because I already have lavish skills installed.

277
00:25:53,326 --> 00:25:59,620
My Cloud will automatically use Lavish for this type of question, which is why I had to tell it not to do that right now.

278
00:26:01,038 --> 00:26:02,660
Okay, Cloud is doing its work.

279
00:26:02,958 --> 00:26:04,868
Now cloud has come back with a response.

280
00:26:05,678 --> 00:26:12,772
Sometimes it will use its plan mode, or sometimes I will ask it to write down the plan in the markdown file, but it's more or less the same.

281
00:26:13,166 --> 00:26:15,396
It's a wall of text I now have to read through.

282
00:26:15,822 --> 00:26:21,796
It's not very easy to understand what each option is actually gonna look like.

283
00:26:22,478 --> 00:26:28,548
And if I'm not happy with some parts of it, I can't very easily tell Claude which parts I'm talking about.

284
00:26:29,038 --> 00:26:32,484
I can select a piece of text in the plan and say this is wrong.

285
00:26:32,718 --> 00:26:35,328
Now let's try the exact same prompt with lavish.

286
00:26:35,502 --> 00:26:36,368
Here it goes again.

287
00:26:36,590 --> 00:26:45,408
I actually don't have to say use lavish because the agent already has the lavish skill that tells the agent for this type of planning it should use lavish.

288
00:26:45,518 --> 00:26:48,004
But for demo purpose, I just want it to be explicit.

289
00:26:48,910 --> 00:26:56,452
Cloud would roughly do the same things to figure out the options, except at the end it would not print out that wall of text again.

290
00:26:56,718 --> 00:26:59,648
It will launch the browser and show me this page.

291
00:26:59,918 --> 00:27:00,676
Now look at this.

292
00:27:01,166 --> 00:27:02,628
This is the Lavish Editor.

293
00:27:03,118 --> 00:27:07,332
The reason I named it Lavish is that it's richer than a rich editor.

294
00:27:07,726 --> 00:27:12,420
I almost named it Filthy Rich Editor, but that's just not the best sounding name.

295
00:27:13,006 --> 00:27:19,300
Lavish Editor basically instructed the agent to create an HTML artifact to visualize what we need to discuss.

296
00:27:20,046 --> 00:27:27,844
It always uses the same design system as the current project being worked on, so this is consistent with how the hybrids app actually looks.

297
00:27:28,558 --> 00:27:31,620
This makes it very easy to review concepts and prototypes.

298
00:27:32,110 --> 00:27:33,636
See the option is laid out here.

299
00:27:34,094 --> 00:27:39,748
This is so much easier to understand than the huge wall of text we were looking at earlier in the terminal, right?

300
00:27:40,526 --> 00:27:46,692
I can also annotate and make comments on specific parts of the artifact to give feedback to the agent.

301
00:27:47,310 --> 00:27:51,492
This is something that's really hard to do with the wall of text or a markdown file.

302
00:27:52,750 --> 00:27:56,068
And at the bottom, there are things for me to decide on.

303
00:27:56,878 --> 00:28:00,324
And I can just click on these options to make the decisions.

304
00:28:00,782 --> 00:28:06,500
And I just send this feedback back to the agents inside of Lavish without even having to go back to the terminal.

305
00:28:06,734 --> 00:28:10,116
Honestly, I can never go back to reading text in the terminal anymore.

306
00:28:10,318 --> 00:28:12,292
This is just like way too much more efficient.

307
00:28:12,654 --> 00:28:19,012
Now the agent has made updates to the HTML and I feel happy about this, so I'll just tell the agent to start building.

308
00:28:20,048 --> 00:28:23,236
start building and we'll end the session.

309
00:28:23,726 --> 00:28:29,968
We can then go back to the terminal now and see the agent will start to work on the implementation.

310
00:28:30,222 --> 00:28:33,408
Because we already clarified all the requirements in the planning phase.

311
00:28:33,488 --> 00:28:36,996
I typically don't need to interfere at all during this implementation phase.

312
00:28:37,358 --> 00:28:39,716
I only come back to this when the agent has done.

313
00:28:39,982 --> 00:28:44,740
And when the agent says it's done, that's actually when things get really tricky.

314
00:28:45,262 --> 00:28:50,372
This is where a lot of people will spin up their editor and start reviewing the diff.

315
00:28:51,374 --> 00:29:00,548
The problem is, AI writes code so fast, and if every piece of code requires your review, then you are creating a big bottleneck on yourself.

316
00:29:00,974 --> 00:29:05,892
Because you can only review so many diffs every day, your velocity will be hardcapped by it.

317
00:29:06,702 --> 00:29:10,564
And even more importantly, reviewing diff is just not fun.

318
00:29:11,118 --> 00:29:15,364
No one says I became an engineer because I love reviewing diffs all day.

319
00:29:15,982 --> 00:29:26,116
My advice here is that in order to really scale ourselves with AI, we have to think of ourselves more as an engineering manager or engineering director.

320
00:29:26,670 --> 00:29:38,276
Your directors most likely don't review any PRs, yet they can influence the quality of their team's software by creating good culture and processes and rely on the team to carry them out.

321
00:29:38,830 --> 00:29:40,356
That's what we should do with AI.

322
00:29:40,974 --> 00:29:47,716
What I do here when the agent says the work is done is not to start reviewing the diffs or start manually testing the changes.

323
00:29:48,270 --> 00:29:50,020
That's too much overhead on myself.

324
00:29:50,542 --> 00:29:55,888
I sent the change into a pipeline I built called No Mistakes.

325
00:29:57,486 --> 00:29:59,748
No mistakes, is also free and open source.

326
00:30:00,174 --> 00:30:07,332
It orchestrates your agent to execute a series of steps that takes this first passcode all the way through to a clean PR.

327
00:30:07,790 --> 00:30:19,632
It would first create a branch if one doesn't exist yet, and then create a commit, and then take it through a pipeline in an isolated work tree so nothing during the validation would affect your current repo.

328
00:30:19,712 --> 00:30:36,356
It would first understand your real intent behind the change by analyzing your agent session, then rebase the change on top of the latest main branch on remote origin and resolve merge conflicts up front, then start an adversarial review in its own fresh context window.

329
00:30:36,814 --> 00:30:38,820
This is where most problems get caught.

330
00:30:39,374 --> 00:30:48,356
And obvious problems will get self-corrected, but ambiguous ones that have product implications will be escalated to us humans for a decision.

331
00:30:48,592 --> 00:30:53,668
After review, it also tries to test the change end-to-end against the original intent.

332
00:30:54,158 --> 00:31:01,636
And this step will actually record evidence that proves the change is working that we can then later on look at to gain more confidence.

333
00:31:02,126 --> 00:31:14,692
It will then do a documentation pass of updating all relevant documentation to reflect the latest change, and also finally make sure there's no linking problems before pushing the branch to remote and raise a PR.

334
00:31:15,694 --> 00:31:27,780
The no mistakes pipeline will also keep babysitting the PR until it's merged, because during the PR phase, we can still have merge conflicts that come in or CI pipeline failures that are very annoying as well.

335
00:31:28,366 --> 00:31:32,352
With no mistakes doing the babysitting, we don't have to waste our own time at all.

336
00:31:32,654 --> 00:31:35,620
Another way to trigger no mistakes is as a skill.

337
00:31:36,046 --> 00:31:41,476
I can just type no mistakes in the agent and it will do the same pipeline as well.

338
00:31:41,712 --> 00:31:45,828
This may seem very slow, but in practice I never stare at this screen.

339
00:31:46,158 --> 00:31:47,620
I would go spin up other tasks.

340
00:31:47,952 --> 00:31:54,404
I come back only when no mistakes says all checks passed, and that's when I go to the PR and apply my judgment.

341
00:31:54,670 --> 00:31:56,836
Here's the PR from the change we just did.

342
00:31:57,358 --> 00:32:06,340
We can see here it summarized the original intent, what changed, how it's tested, and what happened during the no mistakes pipeline.

343
00:32:07,086 --> 00:32:13,988
We can click to see the evidence from its testing to know whether it's really done what we asked for.

344
00:32:14,766 --> 00:32:21,700
Depending on what the change is, the evidence could be a screenshot like this, a video demo, a log file, or something else.

345
00:32:22,190 --> 00:32:26,592
It's designed to give you the most direct way to see the change working as you intended.

346
00:32:26,766 --> 00:32:31,748
We can also see that the pipeline discovered some problems and fixed them before raising the PR.

347
00:32:32,462 --> 00:32:36,484
This is a good time to audit whether these changes are actually what we need.

348
00:32:36,686 --> 00:32:41,988
If anything doesn't look right, we can go back to the agent and ask for more changes before merging this PR.

349
00:32:42,272 --> 00:32:44,964
This risk assessment here is also very useful.

350
00:32:45,358 --> 00:32:50,948
I basically look at this to decide how much time I should spend on reviewing this change in more detail.

351
00:32:51,566 --> 00:32:54,832
For low risk changes, I don't really look at the diff at all.

352
00:32:54,992 --> 00:33:03,140
Because I have validated time and time again, for low risk changes, any problem I could catch is very likely already caught by the pipeline.

353
00:33:03,232 --> 00:33:05,392
Only more risky changes are worth my time.

354
00:33:05,614 --> 00:33:12,740
This is how I scale up the volume of code changes I do every day through a large crew of agents without losing control on quality.

355
00:33:13,006 --> 00:33:20,548
One thing we are starting to see now is that the place where I spend time is towards the beginning and the end of the task.

356
00:33:20,782 --> 00:33:25,092
At the beginning, I would spend time in lavish to plan the requirements more clearly.

357
00:33:25,582 --> 00:33:28,708
At the end, I would come in and hold a bar on quality.

358
00:33:29,326 --> 00:33:34,788
All these parts in the middle is done by AI, which frees me up to spin up other tasks.

359
00:33:35,406 --> 00:33:43,332
This is a core aspect of how I work, and you can see the more time I can free up in the middle, the more work I can go do in parallel.

360
00:33:44,078 --> 00:33:50,052
So an interesting question now is how do we get the agent to work for longer and longer in the middle?

361
00:33:50,670 --> 00:33:55,364
That depends on us giving them more and more complex tasks that take longer to complete.

362
00:33:56,046 --> 00:34:01,380
But more complex tasks are often not as easy for our agents to complete autonomously.

363
00:34:02,190 --> 00:34:04,832
An extreme version of this is when I go to bed.

364
00:34:04,992 --> 00:34:07,204
I sleep for 7 to 8 hours every night.

365
00:34:07,758 --> 00:34:09,988
How do I keep the agents busy for 8 hours?

366
00:34:10,798 --> 00:34:13,604
This is where I say goodnight, have fun.

367
00:34:13,792 --> 00:34:18,180
It's another free and open source tool I built specifically for long running tasks.

368
00:34:18,432 --> 00:34:19,812
It's becoming quite popular.

369
00:34:20,398 --> 00:34:21,828
It's that simple to use.

370
00:34:22,158 --> 00:34:27,364
Just give it an objective and it will keep going until it meets some stop condition you defined.

371
00:34:27,982 --> 00:34:30,192
Let me show you a real example that I often do.

372
00:34:30,414 --> 00:34:32,260
This is again in the hybrid repo.

373
00:34:32,558 --> 00:34:34,948
I'll run goodnight have fun and give a prompt.

374
00:34:36,398 --> 00:34:41,028
Pretend you are a 7-year-old kid and use the high-bit app end-to-end.

375
00:34:41,518 --> 00:34:44,708
Don't mind the profile creation step which is designed for parents.

376
00:34:45,134 --> 00:34:54,404
In the rest of the app, try to do different things and find the first usability problem that will confuse you as a kid or stop you from knowing how to proceed.

377
00:34:54,894 --> 00:34:57,220
If you find a problem, stop and fix it.

378
00:34:57,582 --> 00:34:58,692
Then rinse and repeat.

379
00:35:00,718 --> 00:35:01,284
Here it goes.

380
00:35:01,806 --> 00:35:06,112
Goodnight have fun is now running in a loop to execute on what I just asked for.

381
00:35:06,478 --> 00:35:10,512
I can monitor token usage here, or how many iterations have been done.

382
00:35:10,702 --> 00:35:13,540
The iterations will be showing up as the moons in this row.

383
00:35:14,030 --> 00:35:16,432
And I can see how many commits have been made as well.

384
00:35:16,686 --> 00:35:21,444
Or I can just go to bed, knowing the agents won't stop until there is no more problem to be found.

385
00:35:21,966 --> 00:35:27,780
When I wake up, I can review a list of commits made on this new branch and decide which ones I want.

386
00:35:28,750 --> 00:35:39,140
I typically use good night have fun for improving on some verifiable objectives, or objectives where I trust the agent to have the reasonable judgment over, like the one we just did.

387
00:35:39,822 --> 00:35:52,672
Verifiable objectives are more like reducing page load time, improving end-to-end test coverage, or like Andre Kapati's auto research, keep experimenting different hypotheses to improve on a metric.

388
00:35:52,912 --> 00:35:55,652
These are all well suited for a long-running loop to tackle.

389
00:35:56,398 --> 00:36:05,348
The recently introduced slash /goal command in Codex and Cloud Code can also do something similar, but Goodnight Have Fun still gives me a better experience.

390
00:36:05,872 --> 00:36:11,792
Because I can set a token cap or iteration cap or stop condition more precisely.

391
00:36:12,302 --> 00:36:20,192
Whereas in Cloud Code and Codex, if I set a goal before I go to bed, I might wake up realizing my weekly quota is all gone.

392
00:36:20,494 --> 00:36:26,116
Goodnight have fun solved a very important problem, which is to keep the agents running for a long time.

393
00:36:26,670 --> 00:36:29,796
So when the agents are running, I'm freed up to do more things.

394
00:36:30,574 --> 00:36:34,752
This is when we level up and start working with multiple crewmates in parallel.

395
00:36:35,022 --> 00:36:39,072
So let's spin up another tab in Tmux and get more work started.

396
00:36:39,310 --> 00:36:40,420
Now here's the problem.

397
00:36:40,878 --> 00:36:44,004
In this directory, I already have Goodnight Have Fun running.

398
00:36:44,494 --> 00:36:50,720
So if I spin up another agent working in the same directory, they will step on each other's toes and cause conflicts.

399
00:36:51,406 --> 00:36:53,828
The default solution here is git work tree.

400
00:36:54,446 --> 00:37:00,432
For those of you who aren't familiar with it, a git work tree is basically creating a clone of your repo directory.

401
00:37:00,592 --> 00:37:06,404
I can create one by typing git worktree add and give a path here.

402
00:37:07,214 --> 00:37:09,220
Now we have to think about a name.

403
00:37:09,518 --> 00:37:14,352
This is when you waste five minutes and eventually give up and just say hi bit2.

404
00:37:15,534 --> 00:37:18,500
Now we have a work tree and we can navigate to it.

405
00:37:18,894 --> 00:37:19,872
So let's go find it.

406
00:37:19,982 --> 00:37:21,792
It's in hybrid 2.

407
00:37:22,032 --> 00:37:31,652
This is a separate directory on the file system, so we can have an agent doing anything here and it won't conflict with Goodnight Have Fun, which is running in the original repo directory.

408
00:37:31,872 --> 00:37:36,452
The problem with work trees is that we now have something to maintain in our head.

409
00:37:37,038 --> 00:37:40,260
I need to remember, oh, I have hybrid 2 here.

410
00:37:40,558 --> 00:37:46,692
Next time I come into this hybrid 2 directory, I would wonder what was I doing in this work tree last time?

411
00:37:47,086 --> 00:37:49,712
Is there still an agent running or is it free?

412
00:37:49,872 --> 00:37:52,068
All of that has to exist in my head.

413
00:37:52,462 --> 00:37:54,788
And there's no way I'm going to remember all that.

414
00:37:55,310 --> 00:37:57,540
So this work tree basically becomes a debt.

415
00:37:57,712 --> 00:38:00,996
To get rid of it, I need to run this remove command.

416
00:38:02,382 --> 00:38:02,884
Remove.

417
00:38:05,998 --> 00:38:07,552
This is just a lot of overhead.

418
00:38:07,712 --> 00:38:11,364
My solution to that is another tool I built called Treehouse.

419
00:38:11,726 --> 00:38:12,672
It's very simple.

420
00:38:13,006 --> 00:38:15,652
I just come into this repo and I run tree house.

421
00:38:16,430 --> 00:38:20,868
It would drop me into a fresh work tree where I can start doing whatever I want.

422
00:38:21,134 --> 00:38:26,084
I can keep spinning up more and more of these work trees by running TreeHouse again.

423
00:38:28,014 --> 00:38:34,052
And if I want, I can see a list of all the work trees by typing treehouse status.

424
00:38:34,446 --> 00:38:37,092
So I can see which ones are being used versus not.

425
00:38:37,358 --> 00:38:39,588
When I'm done, I can just close this tab.

426
00:38:40,078 --> 00:38:41,956
And TreeHouse knows that I'm done.

427
00:38:42,350 --> 00:38:45,316
So it will free up that work tree for future use.

428
00:38:46,094 --> 00:38:52,352
Next time I ask for a work tree, it will try to reuse one of the idle work trees instead of creating a brand new one.

429
00:38:52,558 --> 00:38:54,084
So let's start some real work.

430
00:38:54,574 --> 00:38:58,436
I have a bunch of user feedback from my son's last round of playtesting.

431
00:38:58,862 --> 00:39:02,308
So let me use this first work tree we created and launch Claude.

432
00:39:02,830 --> 00:39:09,636
And I'll say, I remember it's hard for the kids to realize they can press and hold the voice input button to talk.

433
00:39:10,254 --> 00:39:13,380
By default, they just click it and then they will see a popover.

434
00:39:13,870 --> 00:39:18,148
Maybe in the popover we add a label that tells them they can also press and hold.

435
00:39:19,822 --> 00:39:20,900
And I'll enter.

436
00:39:21,390 --> 00:39:35,428
Then I'll spin up a new tab, Treehouse, Claude, and this time I'll say the image attachment drop-down menu should have an action that takes a screenshot of the current app and use that as the attachment.

437
00:39:37,166 --> 00:39:42,384
All right, One more tab, treehouse, Claude.

438
00:39:44,526 --> 00:39:49,572
Our agent status bar right above the chat input is not always showing bot activity.

439
00:39:50,062 --> 00:39:57,508
Look into what happened there and make sure when any bots are in progress, it always displays something that reflects the latest activity.

440
00:39:59,246 --> 00:39:59,588
Boom.

441
00:40:00,206 --> 00:40:02,532
We now have three sessions running in parallel now.

442
00:40:02,798 --> 00:40:10,980
I can keep going because none of these sessions will need my attention anytime soon, especially if I tell them to run no mistakes after implementation.

443
00:40:11,598 --> 00:40:19,012
I know whether they need me by looking at the top status bar, and I can switch between the tabs using keyboard shortcuts like this.

444
00:40:19,534 --> 00:40:22,980
That's very important for managing a lot of parallel sessions efficiently.

445
00:40:23,504 --> 00:40:30,724
That said, after doing this for a while, you will discover that juggling between all these sessions is quite exhausting.

446
00:40:31,438 --> 00:40:39,588
The constant context switch and having to remind yourself what each session was even doing just doesn't feel like an ideal endgame experience.

447
00:40:40,302 --> 00:40:45,764
So I kept pushing the boundary on this, and I discovered that I needed a first mate.

448
00:40:46,574 --> 00:40:52,996
Someone I can talk to as a captain that will carry out all my directions and manage all the crewmates for me.

449
00:40:53,422 --> 00:40:56,740
So I can focus on the big picture stuff, like where should we go next?

450
00:40:56,944 --> 00:41:00,484
Not playing whack-a-mole with this increasingly high number of crewmates.

451
00:41:01,038 --> 00:41:03,812
This is how I level up and truly become a captain.

452
00:41:04,110 --> 00:41:08,464
My first mate is another free and open source project, and it's very new.

453
00:41:08,624 --> 00:41:10,884
The way to use it is by just cloning it.

454
00:41:14,606 --> 00:41:19,584
And then I can run an agent in this repository.

455
00:41:20,046 --> 00:41:23,652
Now I just talk to it and ask it to work on any projects I like.

456
00:41:24,206 --> 00:41:30,224
Let's say I'd like to work on Lavish Axie, GitHub Axie, and Chrome DevTools Axie.

457
00:41:30,384 --> 00:41:31,908
They are all GitHub projects I own.

458
00:41:32,590 --> 00:41:33,636
First Mate is starting up.

459
00:41:34,030 --> 00:41:40,864
And the first time we run it, it will do some setup and ask for some preferences, but it's all through just talking to it, which is pretty easy.

460
00:41:41,038 --> 00:41:44,784
And you might wonder why is the transcription so good?

461
00:41:45,038 --> 00:41:46,948
Because it's recognizing these project names.

462
00:41:47,424 --> 00:41:48,164
Let me show you.

463
00:41:49,134 --> 00:41:55,716
OpenSuper Whisper actually supports this customization through a system prompt.

464
00:41:56,430 --> 00:42:07,908
So what we can do here is in this model menu, in the transcription menu, there is an initial prompt, and we can put in some common vocabulary that we use into this system prompt.

465
00:42:08,064 --> 00:42:10,704
And this prompt is what makes the transcription really good.

466
00:42:11,054 --> 00:42:15,620
First mate here is asking how strict I want to be with the code changes in this repos.

467
00:42:16,224 --> 00:42:19,108
And I want to select full gate to PR.

468
00:42:19,598 --> 00:42:24,784
this is basically gonna be using no mistakes as the pipeline to validate its change.

469
00:42:25,264 --> 00:42:28,068
and first task, uh yeah, I'll describe it.

470
00:42:28,740 --> 00:42:39,172
All right, Now a real thing I want to do is for all three projects, I'd like to add an update command on the COI that will update their version to the latest on npm.

471
00:42:41,454 --> 00:42:43,396
And let's see what FirstMate does.

472
00:42:43,694 --> 00:42:47,652
It realizes that this is not one task, but three parallel tasks.

473
00:42:48,206 --> 00:42:51,972
And it's now spinning up these tabs in Tmux just like we would.

474
00:42:52,144 --> 00:42:59,300
Behind the scenes, it would also call Treehouse to create work trees and then run an agent in that work tree to get the work done.

475
00:43:00,174 --> 00:43:04,704
And then it will run no mistakes to validate the change and get the PRs ready for us to review.

476
00:43:04,878 --> 00:43:08,068
Now you can see it's first mate that is doing the juggling.

477
00:43:08,304 --> 00:43:10,384
I don't need to worry about any of this now.

478
00:43:10,606 --> 00:43:12,420
I can just keep giving it more work.

479
00:43:13,710 --> 00:43:21,732
Hey first mate, let's also look at the most recent three open issues in Lavish Axie repo and let's discuss which ones are actionable.

480
00:43:23,854 --> 00:43:24,224
Boom.

481
00:43:24,590 --> 00:43:31,076
First mate now is pulling the open issues from the repo while waiting for the three background agents working in parallel.

482
00:43:31,344 --> 00:43:35,268
Alright, first mate said number 87 is cleanest.

483
00:43:35,744 --> 00:43:36,772
it's very actionable.

484
00:43:37,184 --> 00:43:39,556
And let me just see what is this.

485
00:43:40,224 --> 00:43:42,148
don't toggle in annotation mode.

486
00:43:44,334 --> 00:43:45,636
okay, that's a clear bug.

487
00:43:46,144 --> 00:43:46,500
Alright.

488
00:43:47,534 --> 00:43:50,116
First mate, let's address number 87.

489
00:43:52,366 --> 00:43:58,692
Look, now Firstmate is juggling a lot of tasks for me that I otherwise would have to manage by myself.

490
00:43:59,214 --> 00:44:06,244
Watching it context switch is actually an oddly satisfying experience because I know that's what I would have to do otherwise.

491
00:44:07,150 --> 00:44:13,444
Firstmate is basically all my tools coming together as one cohesive workflow, and I have been really happy with it.

492
00:44:13,838 --> 00:44:17,956
It's been a pretty significant improvement to my overall experience working with agents.

493
00:44:18,414 --> 00:44:23,684
I highly recommend trying it out if you are still directly talking to every single agent session one by one.

494
00:44:23,950 --> 00:44:25,796
It will be a pretty massive upgrade.

495
00:44:26,158 --> 00:44:35,716
Something you'll start to notice after having a first mate is that because first mate took care of so many things for you, you start to run out of ideas for what to ask it to do.

496
00:44:36,270 --> 00:44:43,588
This is a good thing because it indicates the bottleneck is shifting, but it also means you as the captain needs to keep up.

497
00:44:44,302 --> 00:44:59,620
This requires a mindset shift of focusing more of your energy on understanding what matters by talking to your users, understanding the competitive landscape, and crafting a good treasure map that can lead your crew to a good direction.

498
00:45:00,398 --> 00:45:07,492
Once you started doing that, congratulations, you have successfully transitioned from a sailor into a great captain.

499
00:45:07,854 --> 00:45:15,364
Alright, we have gone from not having a ship to being a captain that has a first mate and a big crew that sail together.

500
00:45:15,854 --> 00:45:18,244
This is a pretty good time to wrap up this video.

501
00:45:18,766 --> 00:45:23,172
All my tools can be found on my GitHub and will be linked in the description below.

502
00:45:23,598 --> 00:45:25,060
They are all free and open source.

503
00:45:25,454 --> 00:45:32,740
I built them because I just want to see more people learning how to do agentic engineering effectively and doing it in an enjoyable way.

504
00:45:33,198 --> 00:45:35,524
And that's what I hope you can get out of this video.

505
00:45:36,206 --> 00:45:43,300
I will continue to share more of my workflow and things I find useful on my channel, so don't forget to subscribe if you don't want to miss anything.

